# Model Architecture

## 1. Model Overview

The project uses a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) to classify ECG heartbeat signals.

The model receives a sequence of 187 ECG values and predicts one of five heartbeat classes.

## 2. Input Layer

The input shape of the model is:

(187, 1)

187 represents the number of ECG signal values.

1 represents the ECG feature at each time step.

## 3. RNN Layer

The RNN processes the ECG sequence step by step.

It learns patterns and relationships between consecutive ECG signal values.

The recurrent layer helps the model capture temporal information from the heartbeat signal.

## 4. Dropout Layer

A dropout layer is used to reduce overfitting.

During training, some neurons are randomly ignored so that the model does not depend too heavily on specific features.

## 5. Dense Layer

The extracted features from the RNN are passed to a fully connected dense layer.

This layer learns higher-level patterns that are useful for distinguishing between heartbeat classes.

## 6. Output Layer

The final layer contains five output units.

Each output corresponds to one heartbeat class:

0 - Normal

1 - Supraventricular ectopic beat

2 - Ventricular ectopic beat

3 - Fusion beat

4 - Unknown beat

A softmax activation function is used to convert the outputs into class probabilities.

## 7. Model Flow

ECG Signal

   ↓

Input Layer

   ↓

RNN Layer

   ↓

Dropout

   ↓

Dense Layer

   ↓

Output Layer

   ↓

Heartbeat Class

## 8. Loss Function

Categorical cross-entropy is used as the loss function for multi-class classification.

The loss measures the difference between the actual heartbeat class and the class predicted by the model.

## 9. Optimizer

The Adam optimizer is used to update the model weights during training.

Adam provides adaptive learning rates and is commonly used for training neural networks.

## 10. Model Prediction

For each ECG heartbeat, the model produces probabilities for all five classes.

The class with the highest probability is selected as the predicted heartbeat class.

## 11. Model Evaluation

The model is evaluated using:

- Accuracy
- Precision
- Recall
- F1-score
- Confusion matrix

These metrics help evaluate performance across all heartbeat classes, especially the minority classes.
